have you ever been driving somewhere and  suddenly realized that you were thinking  some strange thought like what birthday  balloons smell like maybe remembering a  note that you wrote to your fifth-grade  pen power something that you wish you  had said earlier on in the day basically  do you ever have a wandering mind  it can kind of feel like the opposite of  structured thought as if your brain's  GPS is on the fritz  just taking turns at random and it can  be particularly frustrating when you're  trying to focus on a problem or an idea  and two minutes later you catch yourself  wondering what it's like to be a cloud  or who invented pockets but if you  search around a bit you start hearing  stories like this one  Amy Baxter a doctor spent years looking  for ways to use cold temperatures to  help kids get through the pain of  vaccinations coming home after an  all-night shift she noticed that her  vibrating steering wheel made her hands  numb and suddenly she realized that if  you combine vibration and cold together  you could ease the pain of a shot and  afterwards she said after an overnight  shift your mind is expansive connections  are made that wouldn't otherwise happen  she basically gave credit to the fact  that she was tired in the history of  ideas this pattern is familiar intense  thinking over a long period of time with  no clear results and then in a single  moment when the minds relaxed and  drifting a perfect solution just pops in  sometimes it can happen in a bathtub  that's when Archimedes figured out how  to measure density using displaced water  or it can happen relaxing under a tree  that's when Sir Isaac Newton observed  that famous falling Apple had a sudden  understanding of gravitational force  even Albert Einstein once asked why is  it I always get my best ideas while  shaving and this can seem all a bit  accidental hard to recreate after all  how many times can you shave in a day  but it turns out that many creative  people actively search for ways to get  their brains into that wandering  the inventor Thomas Edison would try to  catch his mind and hypnagogia that weird  place between wakefulness and sleep he  would fall asleep in a chair holding  steel ball bearings in his hands at the  moment that he drifted off to sleep the  balls would hit the floor and he would  wake up sometimes with an answer to a  nagging question some inventors use  Transcendental Meditation others go on  long bike rides or wander the halls of  the museum and sometimes the key might  be something as simple as  procrastinating cleaning a bathroom or  reorganizing books in fact it's been  shown that many successful artists and  scientists procrastinate on purpose they  don't do it aim lessly though they put  off making something because they  understand the process that things have  to stew in your mind for a bit before  the connections can be made all of this  might seem a little time consuming and  messy not the step by step logical  thought process we usually equate with  genius but some of the ingredients of  genius might be just that time and  messiness so the next time you catch  yourself thinking about how balloons  smell or how rainbows always seem so far  away  don't beat yourself up you might just be  moments away from wandering into the  next great idea
 

